\section{appendix a}  
Here we solely concentrate on the coupling segment of the Hamiltonian and commence with a straightforward scenario. First it is simple to express the Hamiltonian \cite{Hutter} for merely two charge qubits as  
\begin{eqnarray}  
H_c = -\frac{L}{4} (I_x\sigma_1^x+I_y\sigma_1^y)(I_x\sigma_2^x+I_y\sigma_2^y)  
\end{eqnarray}  
with $I_x, I_y$ characterized as in ~(\ref{BI}). In the instance of three qubits, the loop in the center can be perceived as a parallel connection of two inductive coils with a mutual inductance $M$ among them. In order to obtain a small magnitude of the equivalent inductance, the two identical inductive coils are intentionally connected in an opposite configuration. We also establish an applicable presumption that the magnitudes of $M$ and $L$ are very close. Then the equivalent inductance $L_e=\frac{L^2-M^2}{2L+2M}$ would be significantly small. It is also recognized that as the count of connected inductive coils increases, the magnitude of $L_e$ would be progressively lower. Thus it is easy to ascertain that any non-nearest-neighbor coupling between the qubits is negligible.

