\section{$H_F$ for the Periodic delta push protocol}  
Here we obtain the Floquet effective Hamiltonian ($H_F$) for periodic delta push protocol using the truncated BCH expansion. Note that  
$H_F$ for the kicked push is given by,  
\begin{equation}  
    e^{-iH_F \tau}=e^{i\phi_k S^x_k}e^{-iH_0\tau}  
\end{equation}  
Set $X \equiv i\phi_k S^x_k$, $Y \equiv -iH_0\tau$ and $Z \equiv -iH_F\tau$,  
then $H_F$ is given by  
\begin{eqnarray}  
H_F&=&\frac{iZ}{\tau}\nonumber\\  
&=&\frac{i}{\tau}\ln(e^Xe^Y)\nonumber\\  
&=&H_F^{(0)}+H_F^{(1)}+H_F^{(2)}+\cdots  
\end{eqnarray}  
where $H_F^{(n)}$ s can be determined using the BCH formula,  
\begin{eqnarray}  
H_F^{(0)}&=&\frac{i}{\tau}(X+Y)=H_0-\frac{\phi_k }{\tau}S^x_k\nonumber\\  
H_F^{(1)}&=&\frac{i}{\tau}\frac{[X,Y]}{2}=\frac{\phi_k }{2}(S^y_{k-1}S^z_k+  
S^y_kS^z_{k+1}-S^z_{k-1}S^y_k-S^z_kS^y_{k+1}-hS^y_k)  
\end{eqnarray}

perturbations.b:
\section{$H_F$ for the periodic pulse protocol}  
Here we provide a thorough calculation of stroboscopic Floquet effective Hamiltonian ($H_F$) for the periodic pulse protocol using the Floquet-Magnus (F-M)  
expansion in a rotating frame. The F-M method yields $H_F$ as a  
perturbative expansion in inverse drive frequency ($\tau$). However, transition to a  
rotating frame automatically ensures an infinite order resummation in $\tau$. Thus finally we get a series expansion of $H_F$ in inverse drive-amplitude ($1/\gamma_k$) where all the terms are resummed in $\tau$ \cite{asen, replica, anatoli1, anatoli2}. This extends the validity of $H_F$ to the low drive frequency regime but limits it to the high drive amplitude regime.  

The Hamiltonian for the driven system is given by,  
\begin{equation}  
    H(t)=H_0+H_D(t)  
\end{equation}  
where $H_0=\sum_i(-1)^{i}S_i.S_{i+1}-h\sum_iS^z_i$ and we apply a local drive at the $k$-th location $H_D(t)=\gamma_k \textrm{Sgn}(\sin(\omega t))S^x_k$ with $\omega=2\pi/\tau$. For open chains, we  
consider $k$ to be a location in the bulk (e.g. the central location as was considered in the main text).  

We now convert the time-dependent Hamiltonian to a rotating frame as follows,  
\begin{equation}  
    H_{rot}(t)=W^{\dagger}H(t)W-i W^{\dagger}\partial_tW  
\end{equation}  
where  
\begin{equation}  
    W(t)=e^{-i\int_0^tdt'H_D(t')}=e^{-i\theta(t)S^x_k}  
\end{equation}  
$\theta(t)=\gamma_k t\Theta(\tau/2-t)+\gamma_k (\tau-t)\Theta(t-\tau/2)$.  
This transformation removes the driving term ($H_D(t)$) from the  
Hamiltonian in rotating frame ($H_{rot}(t)$) and we obtain  

\begin{eqnarray}  
    H_{rot}(t)&=&W^{\dagger}H_0W\nonumber\\  
    &=&\mathcal{H}(k-1,k)+\mathcal{H}(k,k+1)-h(\cos(\theta)S^z_k+\sin(\theta)S^y_k)+  
    \sum_{\substack{i=1\\i\neq (k-1,k)}}^{N-1}(-1)^{i}S_i.S_{i+1}-  
    h\sum_{\substack{i=1\\i\neq k}}^{N}S^z_i\nonumber\\  
\end{eqnarray}  
where $\mathcal{H}(k,k+1)=(-1)^k[S^x_kS^x_{k+1}+\cos(\theta)(S^y_kS^y_{k+1}+S^z_kS^z_{k+1})-\sin(\theta)(S^z_kS^y_{k+1}-S^y_kS^z_{k+1})]$ and we have used that $W^{\dagger}S^z_kW=\cos(\theta)S^z_k+\sin(\theta)S^y_k$ and $W^{\dagger}S^y_kW=-\sin(\theta)S^z_k+\cos(\theta)S^y_k$.  

The stroboscopic Floquet Hamiltonian in F-M expansion  
is expressed by  
\begin{equation}  
    H_F=\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}H_F^{(n)}  
\end{equation}  
The zero-th order Floquet Hamiltonian is just the time averaged $H_{rot}(t)$ over one time period.  
\begin{eqnarray}  
    H_F^{(0)}&=&\frac{1}{\tau}\int_0^\tau H_{rot}(t)dt\nonumber\\  
  &=&\mathcal{H}_F^{(0)}(k-1,k)+\mathcal{H}_F^{(0)}(k,k+1)+h_F^{(0)}(k)+  
  \sum_{\substack{i=1\\i\neq (k-1,k)}}^{N-1}(-1)^{i}S_i.S_{i+1}-  
    h\sum_{\substack{i=1\\i\neq k}}^{N}S^z_i  
\end{eqnarray}  
where  
\begin{eqnarray}  
\mathcal{H}_F^{(0)}(k,k+1)&=&(-1)^k[S^x_kS^x_{k+1}+\frac{2\sin(\frac{\gamma_k \tau}{2})}{\gamma_k \tau}(S^y_kS^y_{k+1}+S^z_kS^z_{k+1})-\frac{2(1-\cos(\frac{\gamma_k \tau}{2}))}{\gamma_k \tau}(S^z_kS^y_{k+1}-S^y_kS^z_{k+1})]\nonumber\\  
h_F^{(0)}(k)&=&-\frac{2h}{\gamma_k \tau}[\sin(\frac{\gamma_k \tau}{2})S^z_k+(1-\cos(\frac{\gamma_k \tau}{2}))S^y_k]  
\end{eqnarray}  
\end{widetext}

