\section{Appendix A:  Overview of findings from finite-size Ising mean field theory}

\renewcommand{\theequation}{A\arabic{equation}}
\setcounter{equation}{0}  

\noindent The presence of finite-size scaling behavior originates from the large-deviation function, Eq.~\ref{fe}, of the order parameter distribution, Eq.~\ref{cw}.  We will contrast numerical estimates for the finite-size scaling behavior of turbulent pitchfork bifurcations, based on standard Monte Carlo methods~\cite{Binder:1986wu, Janke:1996tm, Janke:2002uc}, to mean-field predictions for the absolute magnitude of the order parameter, $\langle\left\vert m \right\vert \rangle$, and the susceptibility, $\chi$, defined by  
\begin{equation}{}
\chi\,\equiv\,N\left(\big\langle\left\vert m \right\vert^2 \big\rangle -\big\langle\left\vert m \right\vert \big\rangle^2\right).
\eeq{defs1}
When assessed at the critical point, $\tilde{t}=0$, the mean-field observables hinge solely on the system size, $N$, specifically 
\begin{subequations}
\begin{align}
\left.\big\langle\left\vert m \right\vert \big\rangle \right\vert_c&= \frac{1}{\Gamma\left(\frac{1}{4}\right)}\left(\frac{24 \pi^2}{N}\right)^{1/4}, \label{fssp:m} \\
\left.\chi\right\vert_c &=  \frac{4\pi}{\Gamma\left(\frac{1}{4}\right)^2}\left(1-\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\right)\sqrt{3N}.\label{fssp:chi} 
\end{align}
\label{fssp}
\end{subequations}
\hskip-0.1cm Near the critical point, formulations for observables can be computed in closed form from the moments of Eq.~\ref{cw} and conform to the finite-size scaling relations 
\begin{eqnarray}
\big\langle\left\vert m \right\vert \big\rangle&=& (N^{1/4})^{-\beta/\nu} G_{m}\left((N^{1/4})^{1/\nu}\tilde{t}\right), \nonumber \\
\chi &=& (N^{1/4})^{\gamma/\nu} G_{\chi}\left((N^{1/4})^{1/\nu}\tilde{t}\right), 
\eeqa{scp}
where $\nu=1/2$, $\beta=1/2$, and $\gamma=1$ are the mean-field critical exponents, and the $G_X$ are universal scaling functions.  We determine that Eqs.~\ref{fssp} and \ref{scp}, with $N$ as characterized by Eq.~\ref{Nval} or \ref{mfmapnp}, detail the critical fluctuations of pitchfork bifurcations of period-doubling maps in the weak noise regime.

