\subsubsection{Smooth Step}  
For the initial experiment, the reference is a dynamically viable smooth step on $(x, y, z)$ that drives the nano-quadrotor from the hover position to the final one, defined as $p^{d} \vcentcolon = (1,{-1},1)^T$. To produce it, we executed NLP \eqref{eq:ocp} using \texttt{CasADi} with a time horizon of $T = 6$ s and $N = 400$ shooting nodes, discretizing the dynamics \eqref{eq:cont_dynamics} using an ERK4 integration technique. The resultant NLP is solved with the interior-point solver \texttt{IPOPT} \cite{wachter2006}. The closed-loop path and input commands for this experiment are displayed in Fig. \ref{fig:smooth_step}.  

\subsubsection{Helical Reference}  
For the subsequent experiment, the reference is a cartesian helical trajectory, generated using MATLAB. We mandated a radius of $r = 0.3$ m, initial height of $h_0 = 0.38$ m, height increments of $\Delta h = 0.002$ m per $\tau_s$, and a duration of $t_f = 15$ s, divided into $m = 1000$ segments. The closed-loop path and input commands for this experiment are shown in Fig. \ref{fig:helical}.  

