\section*{Supplementary resources}  
\subsection{Conical phonon frequency for the smooth peg model}  
For a stable arrangement, the phonon wavelengths satisfy $\omega^2_{\pm}(\vec k) \geq 0$, where the $\pm$ sign corresponds to the two divisions from diagonalizing the $2 \times 2$ dynamic matrix $D_{\vec k}$. As such $\omega_{-}^2 (\vec k)$ reaches a global minimum at $\vec k_c$ and therefore $\vec \nabla_{\vec k} \omega_{0} |_{\vec k_c} = \vec 0$. Expanding $\omega_{-}^2(\vec k)$ around the nodal position $\vec k_c$, we have  
\begin{equation}\begin{split}\label{eq:ConicalD}  
\omega_{-}^2 (\vec k_c + \delta \vec k) \approx \frac{1}{2} \sum_{ij} \left. \frac{\partial^2 (\omega_{-}^2) }{\partial k_i \partial k_j} \right|_{\vec k_c}  \delta k_i \delta k_j.  
\end{split}\end{equation}  
The phonon velocities around the conical frequency are given by:  
\begin{equation}\begin{split}\label{eq:}  
\frac{1}{2}  
 \left. \left(\frac{\partial^2 (\omega_{-}^2) }{\partial k_i \partial k_j}  
\right)\right|_{\vec k_c}  &=  
\frac{\kappa_1 \kappa_2}{2 (\kappa_1 s_1^2 + \kappa_2 s_2^2 )}  
\left(  
\begin{array}{cc}  
s_2^2 & s_1 s_2 c_\delta \\  
s_1 s_2 c_\delta& s_1^2  
\end{array}  
\right)  
\end{split}\end{equation}  
where we let $s_j = \sin k_{cj}$ and $s_\delta = \sin \delta$ with $k_{c j } = \theta_j - \phi_j$ for $j=1,2$, and $\delta = \theta_1 + \phi_1 - \theta_2 - \phi_2$. The characteristic velocities around the nodal position are thus given by  
\begin{equation}\begin{split}\label{eq:}  
c^2_{\pm} =&  
\frac{\kappa_1 \kappa_2 (s_1^2 + s_2^2)}{4(\kappa_1 s_1^2 + \kappa_2 s_2^2)}\left( 1 \pm \sqrt{ 1-   \left( \frac{2 s_1   s_2 s_\delta }{ s_1^2 + s_2^2  } \right)^2}\right)  
\end{split}\end{equation}  
where we let $s_\delta = \sin \delta$. It is clear that when $\delta = 0$, we have $c_- = 0$, corresponding to the softening of the phonon mode due to the line node; when $\delta = \pi/2$ and $s_1^2 = s_2^2$, we have $c_+ = c_-$ and this gives a uniform conical frequency.

