The Edwards model \cite{Edwards} for self-repelling or "weakly
self-evading" $d$-dimensional Brownian motion, with functions in polymer
physics and quantum field theory, is informally described by a Gibbs factor 
\begin{equation*}
G=\frac{1}{Z}\exp \left( -g\int_{0}^{T}ds\int_{0}^{t}dt\delta \left(
B(s)-B(t)\text{ }\right) \right) 
\end{equation*}
with $g>0$ and$\ \ $ 
\begin{equation*}
Z=E\left( \exp \left( -g\int_{0}^{T}ds\int_{0}^{t}dt\delta \left( B(s)-B(t
\text{ }\right) \right) \right) .
\end{equation*}
Utilizing
\begin{equation*}
\delta _{\varepsilon }(x):=\frac{1}{(2\pi \varepsilon )^{d/2}}e^{-\frac
|x|^{2}}{2\varepsilon }},\quad \varepsilon >0,
\end{equation*}
one defines an approximate self-crossing local time by 
\begin{equation*}
L_{\varepsilon }:=\int_{0}^{T}dt\int_{0}^{t}ds\,\delta _{\varepsilon
}(B(t)-B(s)).
\end{equation*}
For $d\geq 2
\begin{equation*}
\lim_{\varepsilon \searrow 0}\mathbb{E}(L_{\varepsilon })=\infty .
\end{equation*}
For the planar case $d=2,$ Varadhan \cite{v} has shown that centering 
\begin{equation*}
L_{\varepsilon ,c}\ :=L_{\varepsilon }\ -\mathbb{E}(L_{\varepsilon })\ \text
and}\ L_{c}:=\lim_{\varepsilon \searrow 0}L_{\varepsilon ,c}
\end{equation*}
is adequate to make the Gibbs factor $G=Z^{-1}\exp \left( -gL_{c}\right) $
well defined. An estimate for the speed of convergence
\begin{equation*}
\Vert L_{c}\ -L_{\varepsilon ,c}\Vert _{2\ }^{2}\leq const.\varepsilon
^{\alpha }
\end{equation*}
for all $\alpha <1/2$, is in Varadhan's words, "the most challenging step of
all and requires considerable calculation". In this note we shall use a
multiple Wiener integral or chaos expansion for an alternative and
comparatively simple argument, extending the estimate to all 
$\alpha <1$.

