For the rest of this section, we remember  
a recent finding from \textcite{FFSS2011},  
that enables us to  
provide the exact difficulty of the \MCOL{OCN}\ trace universality issue  
in terms of its tier in the Fast-Growing Hierarchy.  
\begin{definition}[Fast-Growing Hierarchy] \label{def:fastgrowing-hierarchy}  
    Consider the family of functions $F_n:\mathbb{N}\to\mathbb{N}$  
    where for $x,k\in\mathbb{N}$,  

    \begin{align*}  
        F_0(x) = x+1 \text{\quad and }  
        &&  
        F_{k+1}(x) = F_k^{x+1}(x).  
    \end{align*}  
    Here, $F^k$ signifies the $k$-fold application of $F$.  
    Furthermore, define $F_{\omega}(x)=F_x(x)$ for the first limit ordinal $\omega$.  
    For $k\le \omega$,  

    $\mathfrak{F}_k$ signifies  
    the least class of functions  
    that contains all constants and is closed under  
    substitution, sum, projections, limited recursion and applications  
    of functions $F_n$ for $n\le k$.  

    Already $\mathfrak{F}_2$ includes all elementary functions  
    and the union $\bigcup_{k\in\mathbb{N}}\mathfrak{F}_k$ of all finite levels  
    contains exactly the primitive-recursive functions.  
    A function is termed \emph{Ackermannian} if it is in  
    $\mathfrak{F}_{\omega}\setminus\bigcup_{k\in\mathbb{N}}\mathfrak{F}_k$.  

\end{definition}  

A sequence $x_0, x_1,\dots,x_l$ of macrostates  
is termed \emph{good} if there exist indices $0\le i<j\le l  
$ such that $x_i\sqsubseteq x_j$ and \emph{bad} otherwise.  
The sequence is \emph{$t$-governed} by $f:\mathbb{N}\to\mathbb{N}$  
if $\norminf{x_i} < f(i+t)$ for each index $0\le i\le l$.  
\begin{theorem}[\cite{FFSS2011}]\label{thm:badseq}  
    Let $f:\mathbb{N}\to\mathbb{N}$ be a monotonic function in $\mathfrak{F}_\gamma$  
    such that $f(x)\ge\max\{1,x\}$ for some $\gamma\ge 1$.  
    There exists a function $L_{k,f}(t)$ in $\mathfrak{F}_{k+\gamma-1}$  
    that computes a limit on the maximal length of  
    bad sequences in $\N_{\bottom}^k$ that are $t$-governed by $f$.  

\end{theorem}  

\begin{corollary}  
    \MCOL{Trace universality}\ of \MCOL{OCN}\ is Ackermannian.  

\end{corollary}  
\begin{proof}  
    By \cref{thm:univ-ack}, it suffices to demonstrate that the issue is  
    in $\mathfrak{F}_\omega$.  
    Recall the procedure that, for a specified process $pm$ of a net with  
    $k$ control-states, guesses a shortest terminating route from the initial  
    macrostate  
    (a witness for non-universality),  
    and halts unsuccessfully if a macrostate covers  
    one that has been encountered previously.  
    The time and space requirements of this procedure  
    are curtailed in terms of the longest non-increasing (w.r.t.~covering) sequence  
    of $k$-dimensional macrostates.  
    These are bad sequences where the norm of the initial macrostate is $m$, the  
    counter-value of the process to check for universality.  
By point~\ref{lem:macroprops:norminc} of \cref{lem:macroprops}, such sequences  
are $m$-governed by the successor function $f(x)=x+1$, which is in $\mathfrak{F}_1$.  
By \cref{thm:badseq},  
computing the boundary and executing the procedure above  
is in $\mathfrak{F}_k$.  
As $k$ is part of the input, this yields a procedure in $\mathfrak{F}_\omega$.  
\qed  
\end{proof}

